  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale  \n   The central idea is to use a constructive method based on de Bruijn sequences to obtain a low-overlap step function. While de Bruijn sequences are classically used for binary sequences with ideal autocorrelation properties, their spectral properties extend to real functions. The key insight is that a de Bruijn-like sequence minimizes the maximum correlation both with itself and with its complement. This approach addresses the bottleneck of local optimization by providing globally optimal structures through combinatorial design rather than iterative refinement. The method is limited only by the number of bits and the resolution of the step function, but its structural optimality provides a rigorous bound on the Erd\u0151s constant.\n\n2. Mathematical specification  \n   The function is represented as a step function with N samples over [0,2]. The construction uses a binary sequence \\(b_i \\in \\{0,1\\}\\) satisfying:  \n   - The sum of \\(b_i\\) is N/2  \n   - Autocorrelation: \\(\\max_k \\sum_i b_i b_{i+k} = \\max(\\frac{N+1}{N}, \\theta)\\)  \n   These properties ensure minimal overlap under shifts. The step function is derived by:  \n   \\[\n   h(x) = \\sum_{i=1}^N \\exp(-\\lambda|x - \\frac{i-0.5}{N} \\cdot 2|) \\cdot b_i\n   \\]\n   where \u03bb adjusts smoothness. The correlated complement \\(g(x) = 1 - h(x)\\) inherits complementary autocorrelation properties from the binary sequence.\n\n3. Implementation procedure  \n   - Initialize with a custom deBruijn sequence creation function that handles periodic boundary conditions  \n   - Validate the sequence satisfies autocorrelation bounds  \n   - Map to step function samples using sampling strategy S  \n   - Numerically evaluate overlap integral at all lags  \n   - Use optimization to minimize maximum correlation with \u03b8-parameter for autocorrelation tightness  \n   - Handle feasibility failures with fallback projection  \n   Pseudocode:  \n   ```\n   def create